WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.080
If you’ve ever picked up a book, and found&nbsp;
it’s full of surreal plants, unknown diagrams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.920
zodiac rings, and a language which is completely&nbsp;
undecipherable, you’re likely looking at a copy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.440
of the Voynich manuscript: the book that&nbsp;
nobody can translate. This medieval text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.120
which has been dated to the early 15th century,&nbsp;
is one of the most mysterious books ever written,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:37.040
as even after hundreds of years, no one has&nbsp;
been able to definitively decode it… So for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.080
this entry into the archive, we’ll take a look at&nbsp;
this bizarre book, and see what we can learn about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.440
its history and possible meaning. The name of the&nbsp;
manuscript comes from Wilfrid Voynic, a Polish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:53.680
book collector with very cool spectacles who first&nbsp;
discovered the book at a college in Italy in 1912.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.040
He was understandably baffled by the bizarre&nbsp;
language and images within — and countless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.000
experts have been trying to make sense of it ever&nbsp;
since. Today, the original copy is stored at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.840
Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library at Yale&nbsp;
University. If you were to open the manuscript,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.280
you’d find that the first section of the&nbsp;
mysterious text is dedicated almost entirely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:21.200
plants. Most of these bizarre florae aren’t ones&nbsp;
you’d ever find in nature, and instead seem either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:26.880
completely made up, or impossible hybrids of&nbsp;
existing plants — with the roots of one species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.360
attached to the stem of another, with the flowers&nbsp;
of a third. Only a few drawings can actually be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:39.200
matched with real plant species — namely the&nbsp;
wild pansy and a variation of maidenhair fern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.000
But that’s about all we’ve got. But it’s not just&nbsp;
that plant drawings within the manuscript that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:50.320
are mysterious. There are also strange cosmic&nbsp;
wheels and astrological symbols decorating many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:57.360
of the pages, some of them with stylized suns or&nbsp;
stars. These diagrams seem related to astrology or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:03.520
zodiac constellations, although many of them don’t&nbsp;
match up with familiar models. Astrology did play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.840
a role in plant gathering and medical procedures&nbsp;
during the period in which the manuscript was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.000
likely written, so that could be a connection&nbsp;
to the first section of the book — but it’s hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.880
to deduce too much beyond that. Aside from some&nbsp;
pretty weird art of people bathing in giant tubs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.600
I’m just going to skip over, the third section of&nbsp;
the book has some art of what might be alchemical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:27.600
equipment, but as is the case with the zodiac&nbsp;
wheels, a lot of the art has little resemblance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:34.080
to common alchemical equipment and symbols of the&nbsp;
time. So, what’s going on here? Well, with most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.440
other books you’d just read the words and figure&nbsp;
out what’s going on, but that’s easier said than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:45.600
done with the Voynich Manuscript. The script it’s&nbsp;
written in is completely unknown. There are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:51.440
theories that’s because it’s been put through a&nbsp;
cypher or code by the original author — scrambled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.160
up to a degree which makes it difficult to&nbsp;
unravel. A lot of effort has been expended to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:02.480
try and crack it, including recent work with AI,&nbsp;
but despite decades of trying, most experts agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:08.640
the majority of the book remains unreadable. If&nbsp;
it’s not in code, it’s also possible the strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.560
alphabet is an attempt to write down some spoken&nbsp;
dialect that had no established writing system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:19.680
If this is the case, the text is completely&nbsp;
unique, and therefore almost impossible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:26.960
decipher. Another distinct possibility is&nbsp;
that the book is filled with nonsense, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.640
was designed to mess with people, but we’ll come&nbsp;
back to that concept in a moment… For now — if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.680
we can’t understand the images or the language —&nbsp;
do we at least have some idea where it came from?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.600
Once again, there are a lot of unknowns. There’s&nbsp;
a letter from the sixteenth century which suggests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:49.360
it was once owned by Holy Roman Emperor Rudolf&nbsp;
II, and was then passed on to his personal doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:55.520
Jacobus Sinapius, then to antique collector Georg&nbsp;
Baresch, then to Bohemian doctor Jan Marek Marci,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:01.040
then to scholar Athanasius Kircher, then to priest&nbsp;
Peter Jan Beckx, who placed it in the collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:06.640
where Voynich purchased it. Basically, the book’s&nbsp;
been all over. As for who the original author was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:14.320
some have theorized it might have been 13th friar&nbsp;
Roger Bacon, or 15th Italian engineer Giovanni&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:20.000
Fontana, who created some surreal artwork which&nbsp;
does look somewhat similar. Others have theorized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.040
the whole thing is a more modern hoax, and&nbsp;
was possibly even fabricated by Voynich.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.120
Recent radiocarbon dating of the parchment more or&nbsp;
less rules this possibility out, however, as all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:39.040
tests have shown the book is almost certainly&nbsp;
from at least the early 15th century. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:45.840
regardless of who the author is, perhaps the most&nbsp;
important question is why? Why write this at all?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:54.880
It’s possible this is a genuine medical text, or&nbsp;
something even stranger… but it’s not impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:00.000
the whole purpose of the book was to frustrate&nbsp;
future generations. Even if the manuscript&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.560
isn’t a modern hoax, that doesn’t rule out the&nbsp;
possibility it’s the medieval era equivalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:11.440
of a prank. We sometimes think of past societies&nbsp;
as serious, but medieval art is often ridiculous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:17.840
and seems designed to confound… so it’s a distinct&nbsp;
possibility the Voynich manuscript was made for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:26.640
similar purpose. In any case, it’s a fascinating&nbsp;
and mysterious book. There’s additional research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.240
that has been done over the years, and&nbsp;
I’d encourage you to delve in if you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.360
interested—or even take a crack at unraveling&nbsp;
the mystery. And as always, thanks for watching.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.760
If you enjoyed this entry into the archive&nbsp;
please lend your support, and like, subscribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:51.840
and hit the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things curious. See you in the next video.

